WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:13.660
According to Reuters, from 2014 Amazon built a program to review CVs automatically, rating applicants from 1 to 5 stars.

2
00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:21.610
By 2015, the company realised it wasn't rating candidates for technical jobs in a gender-neutral way.

3
00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:30.360
The cause was the data. It had learned from 10 years of CVs sent to the company, and most of them came from men.

4
00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:38.830
So when a CV contained the word “women's”, as in “women's chess club”, it marked it down.

5
00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:48.910
Engineers edited the program, but couldn't guarantee it wouldn't find other ways to discriminate. The team was later disbanded.

6
00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:54.380
This is C6: fairness, ethics and work.

7
00:00:54.730 --> 00:01:03.650
Three questions. Is AI fair? Who owns what AI makes? And how will my work change?

8
00:01:04.433 --> 00:01:12.193
Nobody ever wrote a rule telling the program: no women. The bias was learned from the data.

9
00:01:12.543 --> 00:01:24.543
Another source is an incomplete test population. In 2018, researchers from MIT and elsewhere tested three companies' face-based gender classification.

10
00:01:24.893 --> 00:01:37.333
For lighter-skinned men, the error rate was never above 0.8%. For darker-skinned women, it reached as high as 34.7%.

11
00:01:37.933 --> 00:01:48.333
So bias usually isn't anyone's intention; it comes from data and how systems are used. The good news is: it can be found, and fixed.

12
00:01:48.683 --> 00:01:57.923
China's rules on generative AI services also require preventing discrimination by sex, age, region and more.

13
00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:09.300
How do we test for it? We can't open up the AI, but we can test it from outside: two requests that differ in just one thing.

14
00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:20.250
Here's a demo. Two accountants, both 35 with 8 years' experience: one is Daniel, one is Diana. We ask AI for a reference.

15
00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:31.000
If one keeps being called decisive and ready to lead, and the other keeps being called careful and suited to bookkeeping, that's a tendency.

16
00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:42.270
Remember, ask each request at least three times, and look at the overall tendency. Don't conclude from just one try.

17
00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:51.150
If you find a tendency, don't leave judgements about people to AI alone. A person checks it again at the end.

18
00:02:51.933 --> 00:02:56.333
The second question: who owns what AI makes?

19
00:02:56.883 --> 00:03:07.803
In 2023, the Beijing Internet Court ruled on a case. Someone had made an image with AI, adjusting the prompts and settings again and again.

20
00:03:08.153 --> 00:03:21.913
The court found that the image reflected his intellectual input, so he was the author; the AI itself can't be an author. Some scholars disagree, and such cases are judged one by one.

21
00:03:22.263 --> 00:03:35.063
In another case, the Guangzhou Internet Court found that an AI platform had generated images substantially similar to Ultraman, and that the platform had infringed copyright.

22
00:03:35.413 --> 00:03:49.333
So when you create with AI: say you used AI, don't copy other people's work, don't misuse other people's images, and keep a record of how you made it.

23
00:03:50.133 --> 00:03:53.853
The third question: how will my work change?

24
00:03:54.403 --> 00:04:05.203
A 2025 study by the International Labour Organization estimates that about 1 in 4 jobs worldwide may be affected by generative AI.

25
00:04:05.553 --> 00:04:15.273
But the study stresses that jobs are most likely to be transformed, not replaced. And that figure isn't the number of jobs lost.

26
00:04:15.873 --> 00:04:27.073
So break your work into tasks. Which can AI do while you check? Which can AI help with while you decide? And which have to be you?

27
00:04:27.423 --> 00:04:31.743
Then write one line: what new skill do I need to add?

28
00:04:32.533 --> 00:04:48.213
Three things to remember. Bias can be tested: change just one thing. Copyright is case by case: say you used AI. A job is a set of tasks: look at them one by one.

29
00:04:48.563 --> 00:04:56.843
A small exercise: pick something you often ask AI to do, and run a change-just-one-thing audit.

30
00:04:57.193 --> 00:05:04.593
Next time, C7: in the age of AI agents, how to delegate, and how to supervise.
